So `with_globals` resolves names with the default module. If I use the default module (which is `default`), I can do `client.with_globals(current_actor_email=email)` and it resolves to `default::current_actor_email`. 

Now, let me understand the access policy semantics in Gel 7.1. Let me look at the Gel documentation that might be cached locally, or test empirically.

Let me first set up the project and apply a test schema to understand access policy behavior.

Let me think about the design first.

### Schema design

```gel
module default {
  scalar type ActorRole extending enum<admin, agent, readonly>;
  scalar type TicketStatus extending enum<open, pending, closed>;

  global current_actor_email: str;

  type Tenant {
    required slug: str {
      constraint exclusive;
    }
    required name: str;
  }

  type Actor {
    required email: str {
      constraint exclusive;
    }
    required tenant: Tenant;
    required role: ActorRole;
  }

  type Ticket {
    required ref: str;
    required subject: str;
    required status: TicketStatus {
      default := TicketStatus.open;
    }
    required tenant: Tenant;

    # unique ref within tenant
    constraint exclusive on ((.ref, .tenant));

    access {
      # read: only own tenant's tickets, and only if actor exists
      allow read if .tenant in (
        assert_distinct(
          (select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email).tenant
        )
      );
      # insert: admin/agent, tenant == actor's tenant
      allow insert if (
        (select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email)
        .role in {ActorRole.admin, ActorRole.agent}
      ) and .tenant in (
        assert_distinct(
          (select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email).tenant
        )
      );
      # update: admin/agent, ticket belongs to actor's tenant
      allow update if ...;
      # delete: admin, ticket belongs to actor's tenant
      allow delete if ...;
    }
  }
}
```

Wait, there's a subtlety with the read policy. When `current_actor_email` is unset or matches no actor, `(select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email)` returns an empty set. Then `.tenant` of empty set is empty. So `.tenant in <empty>` is false. So no tickets visible. Good.

