`(select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email)` - email is unique (exclusive constraint), so this returns at most one Actor. So `.tenant` returns at most one Tenant. Good, no cardinality issue. Actually, since email is exclusive, the filter returns at most one. So I can use `assert_single` or just let it be a set of 0 or 1.

For the read policy: `.tenant in (select Actor filter .email = global current_actor_email).tenant`. Since the right side is a set of 0 or 1 tenants, this works. When empty, no ticket matches. When one, tickets of that tenant match.

